  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot and humid conditions will persist through \nThursday with daily of risk of showers and thunderstorms, some\nof which, could be severe.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA hot and humid air mass will persist over the mid-Atlantic\nregion between subtropical ridge over the south central U.S. \nand an active northern stream jet. As a result of this active\nnorthern stream flow, numerous shortwave-troughs are fcst to\ntraverse the area during much of the work keeping the risk of\ndaily showers and thunderstorms, some of which, could be severe. \n\nTue and Wed look like the most active days of the week due to \nstronger height falls and stronger mid-level flow with Tue\nlooking the day with greater instability and more enhanced\nsevere wx risk. As always, exact timing and location of severe\nweather will depend on location's of outflow boundaries from the \nprevious day.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler conditions are expected for the end of the \nweek, although showers and thunderstorms remain possible at times.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe southward progress of the cold front becomes more uncertain for \nthe end of the week, although it likely drifts south with time. Both \nphysics-based and AI guidance shows lower severe weather chances \nThursday with declining instability. Showers and thunderstorms could \nremain possible though with the front nearby. For Friday and \nSaturday in particular, there may be a better chance of drying out \nas high pressure builds to the north. This will also bring a \ncorresponding cool down in temperatures (potentially a bit below \nnormal) along with lower humidity. The front won't be too far to the \nsouth however, and will likely attempt to lift north this weekend or \nearly next week as a low pressure system moves into the Great Lakes. \nThus, some low end PoPs remain in the forecast each day through the \nend of the forecast period.",
